*======================================================================
* defs.a99 - every address, port and layout constant TXA uses.
*
* One equates file, COPYed by every source, as in The Lantern Coast:
* change a layout here and nowhere else.
*
* CPU memory (M0 layout; the engine keeps it, see the spec's memory map)
*   >2000->2FFF  kernel: VDP, F18A, SAMS, keyboard, text, RLE
*   >3000->37FF  free in M0 (the VM's value stack and frames from M1)
*   >3800->3EFF  variables (RAMVAR below)
*   >3F00->3FEF  return stack, R10, growing down from STACKT
*   >A000->CFFF  the resident program (the probe in M0, the engine later)
*   >D000->DFFF  overlay window (unused in M0)
*   >E000->FFFF  story windows; in M0, >E000 is the SAMS sizing window
*
* AMSSIZ (pager.a99) writes a probe value into the last word (>xFFE) of
* every SAMS page, so for the moment it runs, the last word of each
* window the program lives in is overwritten; it saves and restores
* those words.  The one rule: the code running during the count (AMSSIZ,
* MAPPG, in the kernel) must not sit on >2FFE.  build.py checks.
*======================================================================

* ---- console hardware ------------------------------------------------
VDPRD  EQU  >8800              VDP read data
VDPSTA EQU  >8802              VDP status
VDPWD  EQU  >8C00              VDP write data
VDPWA  EQU  >8C02              VDP address / register (low byte first)
GRMRD  EQU  >9800              GROM read data
GRMWA  EQU  >9C02              GROM address (high byte first)
SOUND  EQU  >8400              TMS9919 sound chip
AMSCRU EQU  >1E00              SAMS card CRU base
AMSREG EQU  >4000              SAMS mapper registers (when exposed)

* ---- VRAM layout: 80-column text mode (T80) --------------------------
*   >0000->095F  name table, 80 x 30 = 2400 bytes (VR2 = 0: valid
*                whether the F18A is locked or not)
*   >0980->12DF  attribute table, 2400 bytes: one colour byte per cell
*                (foreground high nibble, background low) when position
*                attributes are on (VR50 bit 1; the engine, not the probe)
*   >1300->137F  sprite attributes (unused; sprites are kept invisible)
*   >1800->1FFF  font: 256 patterns x 8 bytes
*   >2000->27FF  sprite patterns, kept all zero
*   >2800->3FFF  the bitmap layer's picture (256 x 96 at 2 bpp, 64 bytes
*                a row = 6144 bytes): one byte past it and the F18A
*                would wrap to >0000
* Graphics I screens (the probe's picture view, no-F18A messages) put
* their 32-byte colour table at GM1COL, inside the attribute table's
* space: they never run with position attributes on.
NAMTAB EQU  >0000              T80 name table
ATRTAB EQU  >0980              T80 attribute table (VR3 = ATRTAB / 64)
GM1COL EQU  >0C00              Graphics I colour table
SPRATT EQU  >1300              sprite attributes (VR5 = SPRATT / 128)
PATTAB EQU  >1800              font (VR4 = PATTAB / 2048)
SPRPAT EQU  >2000              sprite patterns (VR6 = SPRPAT / 2048)
BMLDAT EQU  >2800              bitmap layer picture (VR32 = >A0)
BMLPAL EQU  >08                bitmap layer palette select: entries 32-47
PANETP EQU  8                  picture pane's top scanline (screen row 1)
PANEHT EQU  96                 picture pane's height in scanlines
PANERW EQU  12                 picture pane's height in text rows
COLS   EQU  80                 text columns
TXTCOL EQU  >F1                VR7: white text on black (palette 15 / 1)
* Text styles (the engine, with position attributes): normal text is
* palette 14, set to a soft light gray; bold is 15, white; reverse swaps
* foreground and background.  Underline is a glyph set: patterns 160-254
* are 32-126 with the bottom row filled (CHOUT adds >80).
ATNORM EQU  >E1                normal: gray on black
ATBOLD EQU  >F1                bold: white on black
ATREV  EQU  >1E                reverse: black on gray (the status line)

* ---- RAM layout --------------------------------------------------------
RAMVAR EQU  >3800              variables start here
STACKT EQU  >3FF0              return stack top (R10), grows down
WS     EQU  >8300              our workspace
GPLWS  EQU  >83E0              the console's GPL workspace (DSR calls)

F18FLG EQU  RAMVAR+>00         1 = F18A found (F18DET)
AMSPGS EQU  RAMVAR+>02         SAMS pages found (0 = no card), from AMSSIZ
ROWS   EQU  RAMVAR+>04         screen rows: 24 or 30
TXTTOP EQU  RAMVAR+>06         first row of the text window
CURROW EQU  RAMVAR+>08         text cursor row (TXPUT, TXNL)
CURCOL EQU  RAMVAR+>0A         text cursor column
BMLX   EQU  RAMVAR+>0C         bitmap layer X (VR33)
BMLY   EQU  RAMVAR+>0E         bitmap layer top scanline (VR34)
BMLH   EQU  RAMVAR+>10         bitmap layer height (VR36)
BMLFAT EQU  RAMVAR+>12         0 = 2 bpp picture, 1 = 4 bpp fat pixels
PANEON EQU  RAMVAR+>14         0 = pane hidden, 1 = shown
SR14V  EQU  RAMVAR+>16         F18A status register 14 (version), high byte
SR12V  EQU  RAMVAR+>18         status register 12 after VR58 = 0 (PICO9918)
KEYLST EQU  RAMVAR+>1A         last key reported (KEYGET's edge detection)
KEYMAP EQU  RAMVAR+>1C         six keyboard column bytes (KSCAN), to >21
SAVWRD EQU  RAMVAR+>22         8 words: >xFFE of windows 2,3,A-F (AMSSIZ)
NUMBUF EQU  RAMVAR+>40         8 bytes: number formatting
ROWBUF EQU  RAMVAR+>80         80 bytes: one text row (scrolling)

* ---- the story VM (engine) --------------------------------------------
* SAMS pages: 0-15 the identity-mapped 32K and overlays; 16-26 the undo
* ring (M4); 27 the command history (READ); 28-31 a pristine copy of the
* dynamic area (RESTART); 32 on, the story image: story byte address a
* lives in page 32 + a/4096.
STPAGE EQU  32                 first SAMS page of the story image
PRPAGE EQU  28                 first page of the pristine dynamic copy
MAPRGA EQU  AMSREG+>1C         mapper register of window >E000 (A: code)
MAPRGB EQU  AMSREG+>1E         window >F000 (B: data, the first 64K)
MAPRGD EQU  AMSREG+>1A         window >D000 (D: text being printed)
WINA   EQU  >E000
WINB   EQU  >F000
WIND   EQU  >D000

VSTK   EQU  >3000              value stack, grows up, to >33FF
VSTKND EQU  >3400
FRAMES EQU  >3400              call frames, grow up, to >37FF
FRMEND EQU  >3800
* A frame: return page, return PC (window A address), the caller's value
* stack pointer after its arguments are taken, the caller's SELF, the
* local count, the caller's frame pointer; then the locals.  R7 points
* at the locals, so the header is at R7-12.
FRHDR  EQU  12

* VM registers, kept in the workspace for speed:
*   R4 = PC (an address in window A)    R5 = PC's story page
*   R6 = value stack pointer (next free word)
*   R7 = frame pointer (first local)    R8 = SELF
* R9, R13 are helper link registers (never hold handler state);
* R14, R15 are helper scratch.

HDRCPY EQU  RAMVAR+>100        64 bytes: the story header, copied at load
H.OBJS EQU  HDRCPY+16          object table offset
H.NOBJ EQU  HDRCPY+18          object count
H.GLOB EQU  HDRCPY+22          globals offset
H.ARRS EQU  HDRCPY+24          array directory offset
H.ABBR EQU  HDRCPY+32          substitution table offset
H.DICT EQU  HDRCPY+26          dictionary offset
H.ENTR EQU  HDRCPY+34          Main's packed address
H.CSUM EQU  HDRCPY+36          checksum
H.DYN  EQU  HDRCPY+14          dynamic area length
H.LENH EQU  HDRCPY+10          image length, high word
H.LENL EQU  HDRCPY+12          image length, low word
H.TITL EQU  HDRCPY+46          16-byte title
H.PROP EQU  HDRCPY+62          proper attribute (byte), then plural

CURBH  EQU  RAMVAR+>140        base (>p000) of the story page in window B (>FFFF = none)
FREND  EQU  RAMVAR+>142        end of the current frame (next frame's start)
TPAGE  EQU  RAMVAR+>144        text reader: story page in window D
TPTR   EQU  RAMVAR+>146        text reader: address in window D
ABPTR  EQU  RAMVAR+>148        substitution being printed (story address)
LLEN   EQU  RAMVAR+>14A        characters in the output line
CROW   EQU  RAMVAR+>14C        screen row the next line goes on
MORECT EQU  RAMVAR+>14E        lines since the last pause ([MORE])
SEED   EQU  RAMVAR+>150        random number generator state
ERRN1  EQU  RAMVAR+>152        numbers for a run-time error message
ERRN2  EQU  RAMVAR+>154
ERRN3  EQU  RAMVAR+>156
NEWSLF EQU  RAMVAR+>158        SELF for the routine being called (-1 = keep)
TMP1   EQU  RAMVAR+>15A        handler scratch words
TMP2   EQU  RAMVAR+>15C
TMP3   EQU  RAMVAR+>15E
NUMDIG EQU  RAMVAR+>160        8 bytes: digits of a number being printed
LBUF   EQU  RAMVAR+>170        80 bytes: the output line being built
CHCHR  EQU  RAMVAR+>168        CHOUT: the character being added
CHBRK  EQU  RAMVAR+>16A        CHOUT: the column of the line break
VT1    EQU  RAMVAR+>1C0        handler scratch (output.a99 never touches these)
VT2    EQU  RAMVAR+>1C2        PROPF: the property's kind bits
VT3    EQU  RAMVAR+>1C4        PROPF: the property's length in words
VT4    EQU  RAMVAR+>1C6
VT5    EQU  RAMVAR+>1C8
STBANK EQU  RAMVAR+>1CA        (unused; the story source is in storysrc.a99)
CURAT  EQU  RAMVAR+>1CC        the current style's attribute byte (STYLE)
CURUL  EQU  RAMVAR+>1CE        >0080 when underlining, else 0 (STYLE)
ABUF   EQU  RAMVAR+>1D0        80 bytes: the attribute of each LBUF character

* ---- input and TOKENIZE (input.a99) ----------------------------------
RDTARR EQU  RAMVAR+>220        READ: the text array's number
RDPARR EQU  RAMVAR+>222        READ: the parse array's number
ILEN   EQU  RAMVAR+>224        characters typed
IMAX   EQU  RAMVAR+>226        the most that fit
ICOL   EQU  RAMVAR+>228        screen column of the first typed character
KEYLS2 EQU  RAMVAR+>22A        the line editor's last key (edge detection)
TKT    EQU  RAMVAR+>22C        TOKENIZE: text array's first element (story address)
TKN    EQU  RAMVAR+>22E        TOKENIZE: characters in the text
TKP    EQU  RAMVAR+>230        TOKENIZE: parse array's first element
TKROOM EQU  RAMVAR+>232        TOKENIZE: words the parse array can hold
TKI    EQU  RAMVAR+>234        TOKENIZE: character index (0-based)
TKCNT  EQU  RAMVAR+>236        TOKENIZE: words so far
TKS    EQU  RAMVAR+>238        TOKENIZE: this word's first character (0-based)
TKL    EQU  RAMVAR+>23A        TOKENIZE: this word's length
DLO    EQU  RAMVAR+>23C        LOOKUP: binary search bounds
DHI    EQU  RAMVAR+>23E
DMID   EQU  RAMVAR+>240
KEYBUF EQU  RAMVAR+>250        12 bytes: the word being looked up
IBUF   EQU  RAMVAR+>260        160 bytes: the line being typed
FLW    EQU  RAMVAR+>300        FILTERN: the word
FLS    EQU  RAMVAR+>302        FILTERN: s's first element (story address)
FLN    EQU  RAMVAR+>304        FILTERN: elements to look at
FLD    EQU  RAMVAR+>306        FILTERN: d's number
FLDA   EQU  RAMVAR+>308        FILTERN: d's first element
FLDL   EQU  RAMVAR+>30A        FILTERN: d's length
FLDP   EQU  RAMVAR+>30C        FILTERN: where the next kept object goes
FLC    EQU  RAMVAR+>30E        FILTERN: the count
FLK    EQU  RAMVAR+>310        FILTERN: element index
FLO    EQU  RAMVAR+>312        FILTERN: the element's object
FLRET  EQU  RAMVAR+>314        FLHAS's return address
GLOBRM EQU  RAMVAR+>400        480 bytes: the 240 globals, kept here for speed
*                              (>3C00->3DDF).  The story's copy in SAMS is
*                              only up to date after GSYNC: SAVE, undo and
*                              anything else that copies the dynamic area
*                              must call GSYNC first and GLOAD after.
STROOM EQU  RAMVAR+>320        STATUS: the room (0 = darkness)
STSCR  EQU  RAMVAR+>322        STATUS: the score (-1 = none)
STMOV  EQU  RAMVAR+>324        STATUS: the moves
STPTR  EQU  RAMVAR+>326        STATUS: the next free byte of STBUF
STBUF  EQU  RAMVAR+>330        48 bytes: "Score: n  Moves: m", or a room name
HISPG  EQU  27                 SAMS page of the command history (window D during READ)
HISMAX EQU  16                 commands kept
HISLEN EQU  162                bytes an entry: a length byte, 160 characters, a pad
HISCNT EQU  RAMVAR+>360        commands in the history (not >340: inside STBUF)
HISPOS EQU  RAMVAR+>362        the one being shown (HISCNT = the new line)
* ---- UNDO (undo.a99) -----------------------------------------------------
UNDLST EQU  16                 SAMS pages 16-19: the state at the newest snapshot
UNDRNG EQU  20                 pages 20-26: the snapshots' records
UNDMAX EQU  7*4096             bytes of records
UCNT   EQU  RAMVAR+>370        snapshots kept
UTOP   EQU  RAMVAR+>372        the first free byte of the records
UDOFF  EQU  RAMVAR+>374        the record byte being read or written
URPG   EQU  RAMVAR+>376        the record page window D shows (-1 none)
UPOS   EQU  RAMVAR+>378        the dynamic-area byte being encoded or applied
UZRUN  EQU  RAMVAR+>37A        zero bytes waiting to be written as a skip
ULITN  EQU  RAMVAR+>37C        literal bytes waiting in ULIT
UPAGE  EQU  RAMVAR+>37E        the dynamic-area page mapped (-1 none)
UTMP   EQU  RAMVAR+>380        scratch
UTMP2  EQU  RAMVAR+>382        scratch
ULIT   EQU  RAMVAR+>390        112 bytes: literal bytes waiting (written in runs of up to 112)
URECS  EQU  RAMVAR+>5E0        256 bytes: where each snapshot's record starts (128 words)
